  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts for the desert canyon at dusk. It typechecks, but I couldn't run it or build it with `vite build`, so I haven't seen a rendered frame.\n\n- **Terrain:** One procedural heightfield with vertex colours: a winding sandy riverbed, walls in six sandstone layers, and a plateau on top. It repeats exactly every 192 m, and two copies are placed back to back so the camera can loop without a visible jump.\n- **Lighting:** A low orange sun ahead of the camera and slightly to the left casts long soft cascaded shadows. A violet and orange hemisphere light and a neutral environment light fill the shade.\n- **Sky:** A gradient with a blue-violet zenith, an orange horizon, and magenta and violet bands.\n- **Haze and post:** A mauve fog blends distant walls into the horizon colour. I also added a light bloom and vignette.\n- **Props:** About 34 boulders per tile and 30 dead shrubs per tile (branches and twigs), placed away from the camera path.\n- **Camera:** It flies at 5 m/s along the canyon centreline at about 1.9 m eye height. It drifts left and right about 2.4 m, looks 30 m ahead, and loops every 38 s with no user input. It is moved each frame with `app.camera.setPose`.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set on the first animation frame after `app.ready()`. I used `.then` instead of top-level await, so the default `vite build` target should accept it.\n\nTwo things I couldn't confirm without running it:\n- **Camera movement:** If `app.camera` were undefined at runtime, the camera would stay at its starting pose. The docs say the camera extension is default-on, so I expect it to move.\n- **Visual balance:** The light intensities, fog density and wall height are guesses and may need tuning once someone sees a frame.\n\n",
